# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# discord-ai-feature-builder - environment
#
#   cp .env.example .env      then fill in the seven required values below.
#   npm start                 reads .env via `node --env-file-if-exists=.env`.
#
# This file lists EVERY variable in `EnvSchema` (src/core/config.ts) and nothing else. There is
# no other source of configuration: `process.env` is read exactly once, at boot, and a missing
# or malformed value is a readable failure and exit code 1 - never a 401 you retry for two days.
#
# Anything commented out below uses the default shown. A commented-out variable and an EMPTY
# one are NOT the same thing: `DISCORD_DEV_GUILD_ID=` is the empty string, which fails the
# snowflake check, while omitting the line entirely means "not set". When in doubt, delete the
# line rather than blanking the value.
#
# ONE THING THAT WILL SILENTLY BREAK THE PRODUCT IF YOU SKIP IT - read it now:
#
#     APPROVAL_HMAC_KEY MUST EXIST IN TWO PLACES, WITH THE SAME VALUE. Here, and as a GitHub
#     Actions secret named APPROVAL_HMAC_KEY at the REPOSITORY level
#     (Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Repository secrets).
#
#     NOT an environment secret. The approval gate runs inside whichever environment the build
#     selected, and once anyone uses /credentials that is `user-<discord-id>` rather than
#     `ai-agent`. A key stored only on `ai-agent` is EMPTY in those runs and the gate rejects
#     every one of them with a key-length error that says nothing about the approval.
#     `.github/scripts/verify_approval.py` re-verifies the approval signature on the runner
#     before any spend; if the two values differ, or the secret is missing, EVERY build is
#     rejected and the agent step is skipped.
#
# AND THE OTHER THING THAT STOPS THE PRODUCT DEAD, which is not a variable at all:
#
#     NOTHING IN THIS FILE PAYS FOR A BUILD. `modules.builder.requireOwnCredentials` defaults
#     to true, so every requester funds their own build from credentials THEY store by running
#     `/credentials set` in Discord. There is no owner bypass: you, the operator, must run it
#     too before your own `/request` will be accepted. See MODULES_JSON below for the switch
#     that turns that off, and docs/SETUP.md step 6 for the walkthrough.
#
#     Your GITHUB_TOKEN therefore needs **Environments** (write the secrets) and
#     **Administration** (create the environment in the first place). Both are REQUIRED now,
#     not optional: `/credentials set` cannot store anything without them, and the bot cannot
#     read an environment to decide who pays, so it refuses every `/request` with a config
#     error naming the permission rather than quietly billing you.
#
#     "Environments" is NOT the permission called "Secrets" - Secrets covers repository-level
#     Actions secrets, and everything here is an ENVIRONMENT secret. See docs/SETUP.md step 3.
#
# NOT NEEDED ANY MORE: the Message Content privileged intent. The entry point is `/request`, a
# slash command, and slash commands and modal submissions arrive as interactions. Leave every
# Privileged Gateway Intent switched off. See DISCORD_MESSAGE_CONTENT below for the one case
# that still needs it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Process
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# development | production | test. `development` is the default.
# Only affects logging defaults and your own expectations; command scope is decided by
# DISCORD_DEV_GUILD_ID below, not by this.
# NODE_ENV=development

# debug | info | warn | error. Default `info`.
# `debug` prints every routed interaction; it is safe (tokens and secrets are redacted at three
# layers in src/adapters/system/logger.ts) but noisy.
# LOG_LEVEL=info


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Discord  -  https://discord.com/developers/applications
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. The bot token. Developer Portal -> your application -> Bot -> Reset Token, then
# copy it once (it is never shown again). Roughly 70 characters; the loader refuses anything
# under 50 as "too short to be a bot token".
# This is the single most sensitive value in the file. If it ever appears in a log, a paste or
# a screenshot, reset it in the portal immediately - resetting invalidates the old one.
DISCORD_TOKEN=replace-me-with-the-bot-token-from-the-developer-portal

# REQUIRED. The APPLICATION ID (a 17-20 digit snowflake). Developer Portal -> your application
# -> General Information -> Application ID -> Copy. It is also the `client_id` in the invite
# URL. Slash commands are registered against this id.
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=000000000000000000

# REQUIRED. YOUR Discord user id - authorization layer L0, the account that can always act even
# with no grants in the database. Get it with Discord -> User Settings -> Advanced -> enable
# Developer Mode, then right-click your own name -> Copy User ID.
DISCORD_OWNER_ID=000000000000000000

# OPTIONAL. A guild id for development. When set, slash commands are registered to THAT GUILD
# ONLY and appear instantly; when unset they are registered globally and take up to an hour to
# propagate. Right-click the server icon -> Copy Server ID (Developer Mode on).
# Leave this line commented out in production. Do NOT set it to an empty string.
# DISCORD_DEV_GUILD_ID=000000000000000000

# Request the PRIVILEGED MessageContent intent. Default false, and the default is the point:
# with it off the bot never receives the content of messages that are not addressed to it,
# which is a meaningful privacy reduction for a bot that forwards text to GitHub and an AI.
#
# Turn it on ONLY if a module declares a `$prefix` message hook. If one does while this is off,
# the bot REFUSES TO START and names the module - a hook that can never fire is exactly the
# silent failure the slash-command entry point was adopted to remove.
# DISCORD_MESSAGE_CONTENT=false


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub  -  where issues are filed and builds run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. A token for the repository below. Either:
#   - a fine-grained PAT (github.com -> Settings -> Developer settings -> Personal access
#     tokens -> Fine-grained tokens), scoped to this ONE repository, with repository
#     permissions: Issues=Read/Write, Contents=Read, Actions=Read/Write, Pull requests=Read,
#     Environments=Read/Write, Administration=Read/Write, Metadata=Read;
#   - or a GitHub App installation token, if you already run one.
# Do NOT grant `workflows`: the design depends on the bot being structurally unable to edit its
# own authorization path.
GITHUB_TOKEN=github_pat_replace_me

# REQUIRED. The account or organisation that owns the repository - the first path segment of
# https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.
GITHUB_OWNER=your-github-username

# REQUIRED. The repository name - the second path segment of https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.
# This is the repo the agent opens pull requests against, so it is normally THIS repository.
GITHUB_REPO=discord-ai-feature-builder

# The workflow the bot dispatches on approval, as a file name inside `.github/workflows/`.
# Default `agent-implement-request.yml`, which is the file this repo ships. Change it only if
# you renamed that file. It must end in .yml or .yaml.
# GITHUB_WORKFLOW_FILE=agent-implement-request.yml

# The git ref the workflow is dispatched against. Default `main`. This is the branch whose
# COPY OF THE WORKFLOW runs, which is not necessarily the branch the agent commits to.
# GITHUB_REF=main

# REQUIRED. At least 32 characters of entropy, shared with the GitHub REPOSITORY secret of the
# same name (see item 2 in the header - repository, never environment).
# Generate one with:      node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# The bot signs `<request_id>.<issue_number>.<sha256 of the issue body>` with this key, and
# `.github/scripts/verify_approval.py` re-checks the signature on the runner before spending
# any CI credits. Binding the body hash is also what stops approve-then-rewrite: editing the
# issue after approval invalidates the token.
# SEE ITEM 2 IN THE HEADER: this exact value must ALSO be set as the `APPROVAL_HMAC_KEY` secret
# as a REPOSITORY secret (not an environment secret), or every build is rejected.
APPROVAL_HMAC_KEY=replace-me-with-64-hex-characters-of-real-entropy-not-this


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage and modules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the SQLite database lives. Default `./data/bot.sqlite`; the directory is created at
# boot. It holds approval state, the job queue and the audit log, so back it up and do not put
# it on a network share (SQLite locking over SMB/NFS is not reliable). `:memory:` works for a
# throwaway run and loses every in-flight build on exit.
# DATABASE_PATH=./data/bot.sqlite

# Per-module configuration, as ONE JSON object keyed by module id. Each module's own zod schema
# validates its own slice and supplies its own defaults, so `{}` is a complete configuration.
# Example (single line - this file has no multi-line values):
#   MODULES_JSON={"builder":{"maxConcurrentPerGuild":2,"allowSelfApproval":false},"tags":{"maxPerGuild":200}}
#
# THE ONE FIELD IN HERE THAT DECIDES WHO GETS A BILL:
#
#   builder.requireOwnCredentials   default TRUE
#
#     True: every requester funds their own build. `/request` is refused - before an issue or a
#     card exists - for anyone who has not run `/credentials set`, and refused again at dispatch
#     if the credential went away in between. The shared `ai-agent` environment is unreachable
#     from a user request. THIS APPLIES TO YOU TOO; there is deliberately no owner bypass.
#
#     False: the pre-feature behaviour, unchanged. A requester with their own credential still
#     pays; everyone else falls back to `ai-agent`, so YOU pay for every idea anyone types in
#     any server this bot is in. Set it only for a private server you intend to fund, and put
#     ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN on the `ai-agent` environment - it ships
#     holding only APPROVAL_HMAC_KEY, so nothing builds until you do.
#
#   MODULES_JSON={"builder":{"requireOwnCredentials":false}}
#
# `/status` reports which of the two is in force, so you can confirm it without reading this
# file: the builder's line reads either "every requester funds their own build" or
# "builds fall back to the shared ai-agent environment".
# MODULES_JSON={}

# THE ESCAPE HATCH. Comma-separated module ids (which are also the folder names under
# src/modules/) to skip entirely. A named module is not even IMPORTED, so this works even for a
# module that throws at import time and would otherwise be quarantined on every boot.
# Use it to keep the bot up while you fix one module, instead of deleting the folder.
#   MODULES_DISABLE=builder,tags
# MODULES_DISABLE=

# Extra directories to scan for modules, comma-separated, relative to the directory the bot is
# started from. Each is read as <dir>/<id>/module.ts. The built-in modules always load first;
# the default below IS the built-in tree and is scanned once, not twice. If you set this, a
# directory that does not exist stops the bot at boot, so a typo cannot silently drop modules.
# A module here may not import discord.js, octokit, node:fs, node:child_process and the like
# (AGENTS.md rule 1); one that does is quarantined and the rest still boot.
#   MODULE_DIRS=./src/modules,./my-modules
# MODULE_DIRS=./src/modules

# Load the built-in modules (builder, help, tags). Default true. Setting it false leaves only
# what MODULE_DIRS names, which means no /request. It exists for engine tests; leave it alone.
# MODULES_BUILTIN=true
